<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Unna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Unna is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, east of Dortmund. A town of the County of the Mark and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, it prospered from the salt works of nearby Königsborn and from trade on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a> road. It later became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining and commuter town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/unna.jpg" alt="Unna" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Smial on de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unna_Alter_Markt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Unna lies on the Westphalian Hellweg, an ancient salt-trading route, at the eastern extremity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> district and about 15 kilometres east of the centre of Dortmund. Trade along the Hellweg, including trade in the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, reached it from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The River Ruhr runs just south of the town. Its districts include the city centre, Königsborn, Massen, Afferde and several more rural villages; Massen and Königsborn are former industrial and mining areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement in the area can be traced back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In the Middle Ages Unna gained significance as a way station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a>. It is first recorded by name in an ecclesiastical document of 1032. Around 1200 the archbishop-electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> invested Count Friedrich von Altena-Isenberg with the fiefdom of Unna, among other estates. Over the next few centuries the town was repeatedly fought over and burned down several times.</p><p>In the 14th century it grew wealthy, with a mint and flourishing regional trade. Excavation in 1952 found about 70 gold coins from various countries, thought to have been buried around 1375. From the mid-15th century Unna was a notable trade centre and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. In 1597 more than half its people died of bubonic plague. In the early 17th century the town changed hands several times in the religious wars, and in 1666 it fell under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><p>Its economy rested largely on agriculture until the 19th century, when better roads, railways and waterways brought industry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> mining began in 1870, with industries that depended on it, and the population rose from about 2,500 at the start of the century to 15,000 in 1900. Königsborn became known early in the century as a spa with mineral springs. Major air attacks in 1943-45 struck the barracks and other military installations. After the war the artisan district that had survived the bombing was largely torn down for modern development, though many of its buildings have been restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Unna is a city of about 59,000 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and the seat of the Unna district. Many half-timbered buildings from the 16th to the 19th centuries stand in the old town, together with parts of the city wall and its towers. The medieval castle houses the Hellweg Museum, a regional history museum. The Centre for International Light Art, in a former brewery, is described as the only art museum devoted exclusively to light art. Unna is twinned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, among other towns.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Unna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Small towns such as Hamm, <b>Unna</b>, Iserlohn and Lüdenscheid grew from metalworking and wire-drawing.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-mark">County of Mark</a></li></ul></section>
